  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWith surface low pressure occluded over the Dakotas, southerly winds \nhave calmed going into the evening as the pressure gradient has \neased. Winds will remain lower than yesterday through today as \nsurface high pressure extends westward with building mid-level \nridging from the west. Under clear skies and ongoing low-level WAA, \ntemperatures will continue to climb above normal to the mid/high 60s \nand even the low 70s. A dynamic upper trough over the West Coast \nwill continue to propagate eastward, and its associated vort \nmaximums will aid in the development of a lee cyclone across the \nnorthern Plains by Friday as amplified ridging progresses eastward. \nAs the cyclone deepens with dCVA and low-level WAA, it will interact \nwith continued surface high pressure over the Appalachians, \ntightening the surface pressure gradient across the central Plains \nand once again bringing breezy weather to the near term forecast. \nSimilar to yesterday, sustained winds of 15-20 mph are likely (50-\n75%) across eastern KS/western MO, with gusts up to 25-35 mph \npossible (30-50%) across far northeastern KS/northwestern MO closer \nto the low-level jet streak associated with the upper trough. Unlike \nyesterday, though, marginally improved low-level moisture transport \nwith the progression of surface high pressure further east will help \nboost RH values a bit, keeping conditions below critical fire \nweather thresholds during the daytime. \n\nThe surface cyclone and trough will lift towards the northeast by \nSaturday, with an associated cold frontal boundary drifting \nsoutheastward from the northern Plains. Ongoing south/southwesterly \nflow in the low-levels will maintain daytime temperatures in the \nhigh 60s/low 70s, as well as increased moist advection ahead of the \ncold front. As the western trough continues progressing eastward, \nsoutherly flow will become reinforced through the development of a \nweak lee cyclone during Saturday afternoon, with greater synoptic \nascent contributing to the development of a line of showers/storms \nalong the cold front. Chances (70-90%) for precipitation have \nincreased from subsequent forecast cycles with CAMs beginning to \nresolve activity in recent forecast runs. The story remains much the \nsame to previous discussions, with continued resolution of an \nenvironment characterized by high effective shear amidst low overall \nCAPE. The high shear environment, coupled with steepening lapse \nrates out ahead of the front and robust synoptic lift, increases \nconfidence of organized convective activity occurring Saturday \nevening with the passage of the front. However, confidence on severe \nlikelihood continues to remain low, with marginal overall \ninstability bringing the intensity of that convection into question.\n\nWith the passage of the cold front, the late weekend will see \ndaytime temperatures fall back to the upper 40s/low 50s as northerly \nflow increases with building surface high pressure from the northern \nPlains. Ongoing advection of cool, dry air from the north/northeast \nwill keep the beginning of next week quiet on the precipitation \nfront as daytime highs continue falling to the low/mid 40s and \novernight lows drop below freezing. By the end of the period, global \nguidance diverges in its resolution of the overall synoptic pattern, \ndecreasing overall confidence in any resolution of precipitation for \nthe long term forecast. Future forecasts will have to monitor long \nterm trends to find greater consensus in the synoptic pattern. As of \nthis forecast cycle, though, the emerging patterns of global \nguidance keep things cool and dry for eastern KS and\nnorthwestern MO.",
